boulder's gate 3 is almost upon us i'm  sure i've said that a few times already  anyway it's for real real this time  since its announcement back at e3 2019  we've been drip fed information about  the return  of the legendary franchise so it's hard  to get a clear picture of what to expect  so in the lead up to its early access  launch i've put together a handy recap  of all the things you need to know  before you jump into baldur's gate three  alright so what will be in the early  access  well this is just step one of the game's  development according to larian they  have boasted that even in early access  it'll have a complete narrative  adventure  full tutorial 600 characters 146 spells  and actions  80 combat instances a crime and  consequence system to hold you chaotic  evil folks accountable  twitch and stadium audience interaction  four player online multiplayer eight  races six classes  and over 20 hours of gameplay that's a  lot of nitty-gritty detail  but what makes it appealing is that it  all feeds into player choice and agency  that is designed to be at the core of  the experience even when you've worked  your way through a run of the early  access offerings there's enough content  diversity to enable multiple different  runs  with multiple characters and multiple  actions hopefully meaning your  playthrough will be different from your  prior runs  and also any others you may hear about  from your friends this early access  experience is also supposedly pretty  dense  bigger even than the original early  access release of divinity original sin  2.  if you want a direct comparison dos 2  had 22 combat encounters versus baldur's  gate 3's  80. original sin 2 had 17 600 voice  lines when it went into early access  compared to bg3 having over 45 000  dos 2's 142 early access characters  versus bg 3's 600  and divinity original sin 2 had a nice  69 spells and actions when it became  first playable  while baldur's gate 3 has 146. now  considering i put over 160 hours into  divinity original sin 2 once it hit  version 1.0  i'm sure there will be plenty to keep us  occupied from the early access version  of baldur's gate 3  and all through its development you  don't need to have played the original  boulder's gate 1 and 2 to understand  what's going on in boulder's gate 3. but  honestly i do recommend them  because they're still pretty great  although from what we've been told the  story of baldur's gate 3  is built off the back of what happened  in baldur's gate 1 and 2.  and if you want a reminder of what that  was you can click the button in the top  right corner  where i recapped the story of both of  them however  while the story will touch on some of  the elements of one and two in some ways  that  old school players will resonate with  and there may be some familiar returning  characters  that's not likely to become apparent  until deeper into the game  which could be outside of what we get to  experience in bg3's early access  so it sounds like there will be some  delicious tidbits of lore for long time  fans of the series to get kick out of  but nothing so deep that it'll be  off-putting for folks that are new to  the franchise  after all boulder's gate 3 is not a  direct sequel to the exploits of  gorion's ward that came out nearly 20  years ago but instead sounds like a  continuation of the world of baldur's  gay and the stories  that are within its setting things like  years and time  are pretty vague concepts in dungeons  and dragons but it's also worth noting  that baldur's gate 3  takes place in the current setting of  the forgotten realms  that means the world we will be  exploring in the early access release is  over 100 years  after boulder's gate 2 shadow of arm but  with all those years between stories who  could be returning from previous games  well we've already seen volo alive and  seemingly well  apparently being trapped in an  imprisonment spell for a whole century  hasn't really had too much of a negative  impact on the lad and remember we are in  the world of dungeons and dragons  where spells ageless elves magical  artifacts  and machinations are aplenty if larian  wants a reason for another companion or  character to be alive and appear in the  game  they'll have plenty of lore to tap into  to make it happen  and if you're curious about the fan  favorite minsk and boo and their  whereabouts in particular  well about 40 years after baldur's gate  2 they were petrified into a village  statue where they stood for  approximately 80 years until a magical  surge freed them from their bronze  prison the berserker and the miniature  space hamster have been adventuring ever  since  so fingers crossed we get to go for the  eyes with those guys  if you want to get a better feel for the  world without playing the classic games  from nearly 20 years ago though  the books wikis videos and forums  surrounding the latest edition of dnd  will be overflowing with lore that will  no doubt tap into baldur's gate 3.  as for rules though baldur's gate 3 is  built around larian's own interpretation  of the 5th edition of dungeons dragons  now what that means is that it has a lot  of the fun bells and whistles that 5v  uses  like its dual wielding inspiration  points its suite of magic items monsters  etc but larian have recognized that not  everything translates well from  a pen and paper tabletop role-playing  game to a huge crpg  based in the same world so instead of  sticking strictly to 5e  boulder's gate 3 exists in a middle  ground where the rules are similar  enough to the dungeons and dragons many  of you may know  and love but are also malleable enough  to create a distinct virtual experience  that's full of choice and fresh  opportunities  like i said if you want to get  acquainted about the world in which  boulder's gate 3 is set  and theorize what it might grow into i  think that reading any of the 5th  edition dnd books or wikis can't be a  bad place to start  and as someone who has played a few real  life table top dungeons and dragons  campaigns  it's been pretty fascinating to watch  what i've learned and had my dungeon  master throw at me come to life  in the previews of baldur's gate 3.  the mind flayers are the main enemy of  you and your companions in baldur's gate  3.  these lovecraftian horrors steal you  away and infect your brain  with a special kind of tadpole in the  opening moments of the game  they've appeared in many of the advanced  dungeons and dragons video games  most notably baldur's gate 2 depicted as  closed away in their own den protecting  an  elder brain and in the drow city found  in the underdark  they are some of the strongest entities  in the dungeons and dragons universe  dominating the minds of lesser beings  and turning them into subservient slaves  previously they used to rule the astral  plane but fled to the underdark when the  gethyanki threatened to exterminate them  we see them as a shell of their former  selves at a fraction of their strength  in the boulder's gate 3 opening  cinematic it seems that the mind flayers  are in desperate times  forcing them to directly attack the city  in an effort to restore their empire  now the aforementioned tadpole inside  your brain is what usually causes  victims to transform into mind flayers  as part of a process called  ceremorphosis however like i said this  tadpole  is special so there's a little more  going on with you and your party  what special means or how special this  brain grub is though  we don't quite know yet either way if  these mind flayers are anything like  that tabletop counterparts expect them  to be some of the toughest sneakiest  most manipulative  and interesting opponents in boulder's  gate 3  that is whenever we get to meet them  while fans are no doubt wondering when  we will see the titular city  of boulder's gate thus far everything  we've seen has showcased the lands  deeper into faerun  as well as much more bizarre locations  like the underdark and the hellish  realms of avernus  if we look back to the very first  boulder's gate game there was  hours upon hours of content to play  through before you crossed the bridge  and  finally made it to the city so i  wouldn't be surprised if it's the same  in boulder's gate 3.  given how iconic the location is it  makes sense that larian would want to  build up anticipation  so that the first visit to baldur's gay  is all the more exciting  we've already seen a great deal of  diversity in the world which serves as a  pretty tantalizing tease for what the  future holds  as the game develops it's also worth  thinking about how dense and huge a city  like baldur's gate could be  something so large and imposing with so  many people shops stalls and stories  is no doubt a massive undertaking for  the team to create  so as it stands although the early  access should provide interesting areas  to explore we have no definitive answer  on when we'll be able to breach  boulder's gates i serve the flaming fist  laryn has said that the team expect the  journey from original early access  release on october 6th  to the full 1.0 version to take around a  year  but i think it would be smart to add a  few months onto that estimate  considering the boulder's late release  schedule shifts we've  seen so far after all game development  is hard and early access timelines are  meant to be  pretty fluid but this isn't larian's  first foray into the world of early  access  the team has previously said that this  model has made their games become better  end  products and as iterations of the game  release over the coming months  players will be able to track its growth  as well as be part of the feedback  process  which larian tends to be quite  responsive to i can just say for those  of you who are choosing to wait and sit  on their dice until the final build  i pray that the journey will be swift  and fruitful  at the recording of this video we are  just a few days away from boulder's gate  3 launching  so we're about to shift our focus on to  what the game contains  rather than all this theorizing we've  been doing over the past year or so  i will be covering the heck out of  boulder's gate 3 right here on gamespot  from the early access version through to  its iterations and finally its 1.0  release  so if you don't want to miss any of that  then make sure you subscribe  to gamesbot.com  [Music]  you
 

